ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow
MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS: [the +9.4pp / +63.2pp / +14.8pp deltas are exactly what prompt-conditioned dual-encoder theory predicts when a query-side instruction trained into the model is omitted at inference — see Wang2022 (E5) §3.2 and Su2022 (Instructor) §4 showing asymmetric query/doc prefixes are load-bearing for cosine-NN retrieval; the magnitude ordering (arctic-embed-m 109M >> s 33M > xs 23M) is consistent with larger models being MORE prompt-conditioned during contrastive pretraining, not an emergent integration phenomenon; the NFCorpus +2.9pp vs SciFact +9.4pp domain dependence is the standard "instruction sensitivity scales with query-doc distributional gap" effect (Asai2023 TART §5)]
CONFOUNDS: [Chroma's 0.7226 vs bare-ST A 0.7292 (-0.67pp) is attributed to HNSW ANN approximation but not isolated — could also be Chroma's normalization/dtype defaults; the "competent user picked a self-describing model" framing is a usability claim, not a measured one — no user study, n=0 on the human-factors axis the contribution rests on; bge-base-en-v1.5 "could-it-fail control" only confirms A==B when no prompt exists, doesn't control for wrappers that DO honor prompts (e.g., is there a wrapper × model cell where the seam closes? not reported)]
ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED: [that "default_prompt_name=null + prompts present" is the COMMON packaging pattern vs an arctic-embed-family idiosyncrasy — only 1 model family tested across the 3 sizes, generality claim rests on a single vendor's convention; that the "competent user" baseline behavior is to call collection.add/query without reading the model card — asserted, not measured; that encode_query is the RIGHT fix (it is per ST migration guide, but no test that wrapper-authors fixing __call__ wouldn't introduce new failure modes for non-prompt models)]
STATISTICAL_GAPS: [no confidence intervals or bootstrap on R@10 deltas (SciFact n=300 queries, NFCorpus n=323 — small enough that +2.9pp on NFCorpus needs a CI to distinguish from noise); no seed variance reported for HNSW/ANN (the -0.67pp in-situ gap is within plausible single-run ANN jitter and not differentiated from it); 3 models all from one family (arctic-embed) is n=1 on the model-family axis despite being n=3 on the size axis; 2 datasets is the stated minimum but both are scientific/biomedical IR — no general-domain (MS MARCO, NQ) replication
RATIONALE: The L1 evidence cleanly resolves committee#1's decisive question (seam is real at wrapper boundary, not inside ST — arctic-embed-s config_sentence_transformers.json with default_prompt_name=null is verified and the A≠B embedding check is dispositive), and reproduces in situ through Chroma's production path. The mechanism is mundane and decades-known (E5/Wang2022, Instructor/Su2022) — the packet correctly does not re-claim it. The contribution sits on the integration-default/packaging axis: artifact self-describes a prompt → ST honors it via encode_query → DB wrapper's default __call__ silently routes through plain .encode() and drops it. That seam is real and reproduced, but from a theory/modeling standpoint the finding is a packaging/defaults observation, not a new mechanism or a surprising prediction. Three concerns keep me off GREEN: (1) single model family (arctic-embed ×3 sizes) is n=1 on the family axis — the "self-describing models with dpn=null" pattern needs ≥1 non-Snowflake family to claim generality of the packaging convention itself; (2) no CIs/seed variance on R@10 deltas, and the in-situ -0.67pp is not differentiated from ANN jitter; (3) the "competent user" framing that makes this a finding-about-users rather than a finding-about-config is asserted, not measured. None of these are RED-worthy — the core seam is real and the mechanism story is honest — but they keep the contribution at "strong honest YELLOW: real integration-default seam, mechanism mundane, generality and human-factors axes under-tested" rather than GREEN. Prompt v001.
